<Base title="Indexes" description="How a relational database finds rows with an index, counted in pages and measured in PostgreSQL 18: index entries and row locations in heap and key-ordered tables, B-tree searches, range scans, the planner’s choice of scan, composite, covering, partial and expression indexes, what each index costs a write, and a map of the other index types.">
 <article class="visual-essay relational-design">
  <header class="essay-header">
   <p class="eyebrow">Databases and storage</p>
   <h1>Indexes</h1>
   <TitleLine type="Deep dive" version="PostgreSQL 18, with InnoDB, SQLite and SQL Server comparisons" />
  </header>
<ArticleBody>

  <p>An <strong>index</strong> lets a database find rows by their value without reading the whole table. Ask a database for customer 42’s orders with no index, and it reads every row in the table to find them. With an index on <code>customer_id</code>, it looks up 42 and goes straight to the matching rows.</p>
  <p>It works like the index at the back of a book. The terms are sorted, so you can find one quickly, and each term lists the pages it appears on. A database index is a sorted list of values from one or more columns, and each entry records where its row is stored. The <strong>B-tree</strong> is the ordinary kind in PostgreSQL, MySQL’s InnoDB, SQLite and SQL Server, and it is this article’s subject. <a href="#types">Other index types</a> maps the rest.</p>
  <p>Indexes speed up queries that filter on a column (<code>WHERE customer_id = 42</code>), ask for a range (<code>BETWEEN 20 AND 34</code>) or sort (<code>ORDER BY placed_at</code>). They also enforce uniqueness and speed up joins on a key. In return, each index takes space, and inserts and many updates have to change it as well.</p>
  <p>Databases store a table in fixed-size blocks called <strong>pages</strong> (8 kB in PostgreSQL, 16 kB in InnoDB), each holding many rows, and read a whole page even when they need only one row from it. So the work a query does is mostly the number of pages it reads, and an index helps when it lets the database read fewer pages than a scan of the whole table would. Counting those pages is how you choose which columns to index, and how you check in a query plan that the database uses the index you made.</p>
  <p class="example-note">The worked example is PostgreSQL 18. The figures use an invented table of 36 orders, six to a page, so every row stays visible, and each section then repeats the measurement on a generated table of 60,000 orders. Where InnoDB or SQLite work differently, the text says so. <a href="#run">Where the numbers come from</a> describes the measurements and the sandbox lesson that repeats them.</p>

  <section id="scan">
   <h2>Sequential scan</h2>
   <p>A <strong>sequential scan</strong>, also called a full table scan, reads every page of a table in order and tests each row against the query’s conditions. In PostgreSQL a table’s pages make up its <strong>heap</strong>, so called because its rows sit in no particular order. The next section returns to that, and the storage article’s <a href="/learn/databases/relational/storage/#pages">Pages</a> section looks inside a page.</p>
   <p>Figure 1 scans the invented table for customer 42. Watch the counters: only four rows match.</p>
   <ScanFigure number={1} />
   <p>The scan read 6 pages and checked 36 rows to return 4. Its cost follows the size of the table, not the size of the answer. On the generated table, the same lookup read all 1,725 pages and discarded 59,940 rows to return 60.</p>
   <p>That count comes from <code>EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)</code>, which runs a query and reports the route PostgreSQL took and the pages each step read. <a href="#check">Checking an index with EXPLAIN</a> shows how to read its output.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="entries">
   <h2>Index entries and row locations</h2>
   <p>An <strong>index entry</strong> pairs a key with a row location. The <strong>key</strong> is the indexed column’s value. The <strong>row location</strong> is whatever the database needs to find the row again, and it depends on how the table is stored. Databases store tables in one of two ways, and the next two parts follow each.</p>

   <h3 id="heap">Tables stored as a heap</h3>
   <p>PostgreSQL stores rows in a heap: pages holding rows in no particular order. An entry’s location is a <strong>TID</strong>, short for tuple identifier; a tuple is PostgreSQL’s word for a stored row. A TID is a page number plus an item number on that page, and PostgreSQL shows it as the hidden column <code>ctid</code>. Order 3, the third row on page 0, has TID (0,3).</p>
   <p>Figure 2 builds an index on <code>customer_id</code>. Customer 42’s rows are on four different pages. Watch where their entries go.</p>
   <BuildFigure number={2} />
   <p>After sorting, customer 42’s entries sit side by side. Finding them means finding the first 42 and reading forward until the key changes. The table itself is not reordered: the index holds the order, and the TIDs lead back to the rows.</p>
   <p>On the generated table, an index on <code>customer_id</code> cut the lookup from 1,725 pages to 62. It read 2 index pages to find the 60 entries, then the 60 table pages they point to, because customer 42’s orders sit on 60 different pages.</p>

   <h3 id="clustered">Tables stored in key order</h3>
   <p>The other design keeps the table itself sorted by its primary key and searches it the way an index is searched, which the next section shows. MySQL’s InnoDB works this way, and its documentation calls the table the <strong>clustered index</strong>. (A table with no primary key uses its first unique index on non-null columns, or else a hidden row ID.) SQLite’s ordinary tables do the same with a hidden integer key called the <code>rowid</code>, which a column declared <code>INTEGER PRIMARY KEY</code> becomes.</p>
   <p>Rows in such a table have no fixed page and slot to point at, because a row moves when its page fills up and divides in two. So the design stores the row’s key in the entries of every other index instead. InnoDB’s documentation says each record in a secondary index contains the primary key columns for the row, and a SQLite entry holds the rowid. Finding a row through a secondary index is then two searches: down the index to the entry, then down the table for the key the entry carries.</p>
   <p>Figure 3 follows customer 42 through that design, with <code>order_id</code> as the table’s key.</p>
   <ClusteredFigure number={3} />
   <p>Compare Figure 2. There each entry led straight to a page. Here each entry starts another search, so sixty matching entries mean sixty descents of the table. The designs differ in other ways that change what an index costs:</p>
   <ul>
    <li>A lookup by the table’s own key is a single search, and rows with neighbouring keys share pages, so a range of <code>order_id</code>s reads few pages. In a heap, rows land wherever there is room, so the key says nothing about the page.</li>
    <li>The key is copied into every other index. InnoDB’s documentation advises a short primary key for this reason, because a long one makes every secondary index larger.</li>
    <li>Where new rows go depends on the key. Keys that only increase always land at the end of the table, while random key values, such as random UUIDs, land anywhere in it. In a heap, a random key scatters only its own index. <a href="#splits">Page splits</a> shows what that costs.</li>
   </ul>
   <p>The page counts in the rest of this article are PostgreSQL’s, and Figures 4 to 7 draw a heap. Here is one conversion. In a key-ordered table the 62-page lookup above becomes 2 index pages plus 60 descents of the table. Every descent starts from the same few upper pages, which the first descent has already read into memory, so what is left is about one bottom page per row plus a few shared ones. Read the later counts the same way, replacing each table page with a descent. (SQLite’s <code>WITHOUT ROWID</code> tables sort by a declared primary key instead of a rowid, and PostgreSQL’s <code>CLUSTER</code> sorts a table once without keeping it sorted.)</p>
  </section>

  <section id="btree">
   <h2>B-tree</h2>
   <p>So far, nothing says how a search finds the first 42. A <strong>B-tree</strong> is a tree of index pages that lets a search jump there instead of reading the entries from the start. The sorted entries live on <strong>leaf pages</strong>. Above them, <strong>branch pages</strong> hold one key for each page below, marking where that page’s range begins; PostgreSQL’s documentation calls them internal pages. The single page at the top is the <strong>root</strong>.</p>
   <p>Figure 4 draws the invented index as a B-tree on its side, with the root on the left. Pick a customer and count the index pages the search reads.</p>
   <DescentFigure number={4} />
   <p>Every search in this tree reads three index pages, whatever the key: root, branch and leaf. That count is the tree’s <strong>depth</strong>, and it grows very slowly. A real leaf page holds hundreds of entries, so each extra level multiplies the rows the tree can reach by hundreds.</p>
   <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-labelledby="depth-caption"><table><caption id="depth-caption">B-tree depth for an index on one integer column, measured in PostgreSQL 18</caption><thead><tr><th>Rows</th><th>Levels</th><th>Index pages</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>100,000</td><td>2</td><td>276</td></tr><tr><td>1,000,000</td><td>3</td><td>2,745</td></tr><tr><td>10,000,000</td><td>3</td><td>27,422</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
   <p>In PostgreSQL’s 8 kB pages, a freshly built leaf page held 367 integer entries, and going from 1 million rows to 10 million added no level. A lookup for one key among the 10 million read 4 pages: 3 in the index and 1 in the heap. The generated customer index has two levels, a root and its leaf pages, which is why its lookup read 2 index pages. PostgreSQL also stores each repeated key once, with a list of its 60 TIDs called a <strong>posting list</strong>.</p>
   <p>In PostgreSQL and InnoDB, pages on the same level are linked to their neighbours, so a scan can step from one leaf page to the next without going back up the tree. SQLite’s leaf pages hold no such links, so at the end of a leaf it moves up to the parent page and down to the next leaf. It keeps the path it came down, so that is not a new search from the root. Either way, a range scan descends once. The next section follows one.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="range">
   <h2>Range scans</h2>
   <p>A <strong>range scan</strong> descends to the first entry at or above a lower bound, then reads entries in order until it passes the upper bound. B-trees serve <code>&lt;</code>, <code>&lt;=</code>, <code>=</code>, <code>&gt;=</code>, <code>&gt;</code>, <code>BETWEEN</code>, <code>IN</code> and <code>IS NULL</code> this way.</p>
   <p>Figure 5 runs a range scan for customers 20 to 34. Watch how many times the search descends, and what it does at the end of a leaf page.</p>
   <RangeFigure number={5} />
   <p>The search descended once, to the first 20. At the end of each leaf page it followed the link to the next, and it stopped at 42, the first key past the range. The entries came out in key order, so the <code>ORDER BY</code> needed no extra work.</p>
   <p>On the generated table, customers 40 to 45 have 360 orders, and the index search read 3 pages to collect them: the root and two leaf pages. The next section follows the 360 rows from there, because PostgreSQL’s plan did not return them in index order and sorted them again.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="selectivity">
   <h2>Selectivity and the planner’s choice of scan</h2>
   <p><strong>Selectivity</strong> is the fraction of a table’s rows that a condition matches. A condition matching 60 rows out of 60,000 is very selective; one matching half the table is not. The <strong>planner</strong>, the part of the database that chooses how to run a query, estimates selectivity from statistics that a command such as <code>ANALYZE</code> collects. Then it picks a route. InnoDB and SQLite also choose between the index and a full scan by estimate, and the idea that an index stops paying once it matches enough rows carries over to them. PostgreSQL has three routes to choose from:</p>
   <ul>
    <li>An <strong>index scan</strong> follows each entry to its row, in index order.</li>
    <li>A <strong>bitmap heap scan</strong> collects the matching TIDs from the index, sorts them by page, then reads each table page once. It saves repeat visits but returns rows in page order, not index order. MySQL’s Multi-Range Read does the same job; its plan says <code>Using MRR</code>.</li>
    <li>A <strong>sequential scan</strong> ignores the index and reads every page.</li>
   </ul>
   <p>The lost order explains the sort in the range-scan example: the bitmap scan returned customers 40 to 45 in page order, so PostgreSQL sorted them again. Figure 6 widens a range one customer at a time. Watch how quickly the matching rows spread across the pages.</p>
   <SelectivityFigure number={6} />
   <p>By the third step, with 8 rows or 22% of the table matched, every page held a match. From there the index can only add work. A bitmap heap scan reads all six pages anyway, and an index scan reads some of them more than once.</p>
   <p>The order of reads matters too. A sequential scan reads pages one after another, which storage serves quickly, while index lookups jump around, so planners price a jumping read higher. PostgreSQL makes that price two settings, <code>seq_page_cost</code> and <code>random_page_cost</code>, which you can change.</p>
   <p>The planner decides from estimates, not from counted pages, and its estimates don’t see where rows physically sit. So it can switch routes before or after the point where every page holds a match, and the same plan can read twice as many pages on a differently ordered table. On the generated table, with only the customer index, PostgreSQL chose a bitmap heap scan up to <code>customer_id &lt;= 500</code>, half the table, and a sequential scan from 550. The bitmap scan read 922 of the 1,725 pages at 500. On a copy of the table with its rows shuffled, the same condition touched 1,715 pages, and the planner still switched at the same point. When the choice matters, compare the pages each plan actually read.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="composite">
   <h2>Composite (multicolumn) indexes</h2>
   <p>A <strong>composite index</strong> has more than one key column. Its entries sort by the first column, then by the second among entries that tie on the first, and so on. The column order decides which entries sit together.</p>
   <p>Figure 7 asks for customer 42’s two newest orders since 10 January. Switch between the three indexes and count the entries each one reads.</p>
   <CompositeFigure number={7} />
   <p>With <code>customer_id</code> alone, the index found customer 42’s four orders but knew nothing about their dates, so every row was fetched, checked and sorted. With <code>(customer_id, placed_at DESC)</code>, the entries were already newest first, and the scan stopped after two. With the date first, every customer’s entries were interleaved by date, and the scan read 16 to find two.</p>
   <p>An index on <code>(a, b)</code> helps a query that filters on <code>a</code>, or on <code>a</code> and <code>b</code>, because entries with the same <code>a</code> sit together. It generally doesn’t help a query on <code>b</code> alone, because the matching <code>b</code> values are spread through every <code>a</code>. PostgreSQL’s <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/indexes-multicolumn.html">multicolumn index documentation</a> states it precisely: equality conditions on the leading columns, plus a range condition on the next one, limit how much of the index is read. So put the columns compared with <code>=</code> first, then the column used for the range or the sort.</p>
   <p>PostgreSQL 18 can search an index anyway when the leading column has no condition, with one search for each distinct value of it, and MySQL and SQLite can in narrow cases. It pays only when the leading column has few distinct values, so don’t design an index around it. The sources give each engine’s conditions.</p>
   <p>On the generated table, the <strong>history query</strong> asks for customer 42’s ten newest orders since 1 February:</p>
   <Sql code={history} label="The history query: customer 42’s ten newest orders since 1 February" />
   <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-labelledby="history-caption"><table><caption id="history-caption">The history query on the generated table with each index in place</caption><thead><tr><th>Index</th><th>Plan</th><th>Pages read</th></tr></thead><tbody>
    <tr><td>None</td><td>Sequential scan, then sort</td><td>1,725</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>(customer_id)</code></td><td>Bitmap heap scan: 60 rows fetched, 45 rejected, then sort</td><td>62</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>(placed_at DESC, customer_id)</code></td><td>Index scan through about 11,000 entries, then a partial sort</td><td>57</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>(customer_id, placed_at DESC, order_id DESC)</code></td><td>Index scan, stops after 10 rows, no sort</td><td>13</td></tr>
   </tbody></table></div>
   <p>Pages dominate the work, but not all of it. The reversed index’s 57 pages look cheap beside the customer index’s 62, yet it found the right ten rows only after reading about 11,000 entries, every customer’s newest first, and that many entries is a sign the index doesn’t fit the query. The composite index read 13 pages: 3 index pages, one per level, and 10 table pages, one per row. Its entries are wider than the customer index’s, so its tree has three levels.</p>
   <p>The <code>order_id DESC</code> column breaks ties between orders placed in the same instant, as the query’s <code>ORDER BY</code> does. Keep the <code>ORDER BY</code> even when an index supplies the order, because SQL guarantees no order without it.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="covering">
   <h2>Covering indexes and index-only scans</h2>
   <p>An <strong>index-only scan</strong> answers a query from the index without visiting the table. PostgreSQL names the plan step that way, MySQL’s <code>EXPLAIN</code> reports <code>Using index</code>, and SQLite’s reports <code>USING COVERING INDEX</code>. The first requirement is columns: every column the query uses must be in the index. An index holding them is a <strong>covering index</strong> for that query.</p>
   <p>The history query returns <code>total</code>, which the composite index lacks, so each of its ten rows needs a table visit. PostgreSQL has an <code>INCLUDE</code> clause, as SQL Server does, that adds columns to every entry as stored values that are not part of the sort. MySQL’s <code>CREATE INDEX</code> has no such clause. There, and in the covering index SQLite’s documentation builds, the extra column goes at the end of the key, as in <code>(customer_id, placed_at, total)</code>. In a table stored in key order, an index covers a little more than its definition shows, because every entry already carries the primary key. A query that needs only <code>customer_id</code> and <code>order_id</code> is covered with nothing added.</p>
   <p>In SQLite, holding the columns is enough, because a reader never sees the index and the table at different versions, so an entry can be trusted. PostgreSQL and InnoDB keep old row versions for transactions that began earlier, so an entry may belong to a version the current transaction can’t see. PostgreSQL then checks the table, unless a bit in its <strong>visibility map</strong> says every row on that page is visible to every transaction. <code>VACUUM</code>, its cleanup of old row versions, sets the bit, and any change to the page clears it. InnoDB has no map. A secondary index usually answers a covered query alone, but when a record is marked deleted or its page was changed by a newer transaction, it looks the row up in the clustered index. In both, a table that changes quickly loses some of the benefit.</p>
   <p>On the generated table, adding <code>total</code> to the composite index took the history query from 13 pages to 4 once <code>VACUUM</code> had run, and grew the index from 2.3 to 2.9 MiB. A covering index is bigger than the one it extends, and changing an included column now changes the index too. It pays for a frequent query on a table that changes slowly. <a href="/learn/databases/relational/postgresql/indexes/#index-only">Indexes in PostgreSQL</a> follows the visibility map in a figure and measures what a covering index saves on the generated table.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="kinds">
   <h2>Partial, expression and unique indexes</h2>
   <h3>Partial indexes</h3>
   <p>A <strong>partial index</strong> holds entries only for rows that match its own <code>WHERE</code> clause. PostgreSQL and SQLite support them, and SQL Server calls them filtered indexes. MySQL has none. A fifth of the generated orders are pending:</p>
   <Sql code={partialIndex} label="Index only the pending orders" />
   <p>This index is 35 pages, against 166 for an index on <code>placed_at</code> covering every row, and inserts of paid orders skip it. A query for the ten newest pending orders read it backwards and touched 4 pages. The planner uses a partial index only when the query’s own conditions imply the index’s <code>WHERE</code> clause.</p>

   <h3>Expression indexes</h3>
   <p>An <strong>expression index</strong> indexes the result of an expression instead of a column. SQLite does it directly, and MySQL calls its version functional key parts. In PostgreSQL, a report of one day’s orders can’t use an index on <code>placed_at</code> through <code>placed_at::date</code>, and that cast can’t be indexed either. Its result depends on the session’s time zone, so the same stored moment could give two different dates and the index could not hold a single value for it. A fixed time zone makes it indexable:</p>
   <Sql code={dayIndex} label="Index each order’s date in UTC" />
   <p>A query that repeats the expression exactly, <code>(placed_at AT TIME ZONE 'UTC')::date = date '2026-02-01'</code>, used the index and touched 47 pages to count 1,440 orders. The same question written as <code>placed_at::date = date '2026-02-01'</code> read all 1,725 pages. PostgreSQL’s planner matches the expression as written, and so does SQLite’s, which treats <code>x+y</code> and <code>y+x</code> as different expressions.</p>

   <h3>Unique indexes and foreign keys</h3>
   <p>A <strong>unique index</strong> also refuses a second entry with the same key. That is why a unique constraint is built on an index: checking for an existing copy of a key is a search in the index. PostgreSQL creates one for every primary key and <code>UNIQUE</code> constraint, so <code>index_orders_pkey</code> is one. In InnoDB the primary key is the table itself. An index on <code>(customer_id, placed_at)</code> declared <code>UNIQUE</code> would refuse two orders for one customer in the same instant.</p>
   <p>A foreign key’s referencing column wants an index of its own. If <code>customer_id</code> referenced a customers table, deleting a customer would have to find that customer’s orders, and without an index that is a scan of the whole orders table. PostgreSQL doesn’t create the index automatically, though MySQL does when none exists.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="types">
   <h2>Other index types</h2>
   <p>Every index so far has been a B-tree. The table below is PostgreSQL’s menu: it has five other built-in types, which it calls <strong>access methods</strong>, and extensions add more. Other engines offer fewer. InnoDB adds full-text indexes, an inverted design like GIN, and spatial R-trees. SQLite offers R-tree and full-text search as optional modules that appear as virtual tables. Each arranges its entries for a different kind of condition, and <code>USING</code> chooses one: <code>CREATE INDEX ON products USING gin (tags)</code>.</p>
   <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-labelledby="types-caption"><table><caption id="types-caption">PostgreSQL index types and the conditions they serve</caption><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Built for</th><th>Example condition</th><th>Read more</th></tr></thead><tbody>
    <tr><td>B-tree (default)</td><td>Equality, ranges and sorting on values with one order</td><td><code>customer_id BETWEEN 40 AND 45</code></td><td>This article</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Hash</td><td>Equality only</td><td><code>api_key = 'k_81f3'</code></td><td><a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/hash-index.html">Hash indexes</a></td></tr>
    <tr><td>GIN</td><td>Values that hold many keys: arrays, <code>jsonb</code> documents and full-text search</td><td><code>tags @&gt; ARRAY['gift']</code></td><td><a href="/learn/databases/relational/postgresql/indexes/#gin">GIN indexes</a>, <a href="/learn/databases/relational/postgresql/json/">JSON</a>, <a href="/learn/databases/relational/postgresql/full-text-search/">full-text search</a></td></tr>
    <tr><td>GiST and SP-GiST</td><td>Data with no single sort order, such as geometry, ranges and network addresses, and nearest-neighbour searches</td><td><code>booked &amp;&amp; tstzrange('2026-10-01', '2026-10-02')</code></td><td><a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/gist.html">GiST</a>, <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/spgist.html">SP-GiST</a></td></tr>
    <tr><td>BRIN</td><td>Very large tables whose values follow the rows’ physical order, such as timestamps in an append-only log</td><td><code>logged_at &gt;= '2026-10-01'</code></td><td><a href="/learn/databases/relational/postgresql/indexes/#brin">BRIN indexes</a></td></tr>
    <tr><td>HNSW and IVFFlat (pgvector extension)</td><td>Approximate nearest neighbours among embeddings</td><td><code>ORDER BY embedding &lt;=&gt; $1 LIMIT 10</code></td><td><a href="/learn/databases/relational/postgresql/vector-search/">Vector search</a></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Bloom (extension)</td><td>Equality on any combination of many columns</td><td><code>colour = 'blue' AND size = 'M'</code></td><td><a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/bloom.html">bloom</a></td></tr>
   </tbody></table></div>
   <p>A BRIN index stays tiny because it records only the smallest and largest value in each range of table pages, so it can skip a range only when nearby rows hold nearby values. GIN is the closest to the book index from the opening: one entry per key, such as a word or an array element, listing every row that contains it.</p>
   <p>The ways of shaping an index that earlier sections taught on B-trees are separate from its type. Partial and expression indexes work with any type. GiST, GIN and BRIN indexes can also have several key columns, GiST and SP-GiST accept <code>INCLUDE</code>, and only a B-tree can be unique.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="writes">
   <h2>Index write costs</h2>
   <p>Each index is another copy of some of the table’s data, so a write to the table is also a write to its indexes. Figure 8 follows one insert and one update through a PostgreSQL table with up to four indexes: the primary key, <code>customer_id</code>, <code>(customer_id, placed_at DESC)</code> and <code>total</code>. Change the number of indexes and count the pages each statement writes. When a new entry belongs on a page that is already full, the page <strong>splits</strong>, which <a href="#splits">Page splits</a> explains below.</p>
   <WriteFigure number={8} />
   <p>With four indexes, the insert wrote 8 pages, four of them for one page split. With only the primary key, it wrote 2. The update wrote 1 page until an index held <code>total</code>, then 5. In PostgreSQL, an update that changes no indexed column, and whose new row version fits on the same page, leaves every index alone, because the old entries still reach the row and the old version points on to the new one within the page. PostgreSQL calls this a heap-only tuple (HOT) update. Any other update adds an entry to every index on the table, including those on columns it didn’t change.</p>
   <p>InnoDB updates the row in place in the clustered index, and leaves a secondary index alone unless an indexed column changed; then it marks the old record deleted and inserts a new one. Changing the primary key itself moves the row and rewrites its entry in every secondary index, because each entry carries the key.</p>
   <p>On the generated data, with just the primary key, inserting 10,000 orders wrote 3.3 MB of write-ahead log, the log of changes PostgreSQL keeps for crash recovery (InnoDB’s redo log does the same job). With four more indexes, the same insert wrote 7.7 MB.</p>

   <h3 id="splits">Page splits</h3>
   <p>When the leaf page where an entry belongs is full, the page <strong>splits</strong> in two: half its entries move to a new page, and the branch page above gains a pointer to it. A split can travel upwards when the branch page is full too, and a split of the root adds a level to the tree.</p>
   <p>Where new keys land decides how often this happens. Keys that only increase, such as a timestamp or an auto-increment ID, always land on the rightmost leaf page. InnoDB’s documentation says pages filled by sequential inserts end up about 15/16 full, against anywhere from 1/2 to 15/16 for random inserts. In a table stored in key order the same holds for the table itself, so a random primary key splits table pages as well as index pages. In a heap, only the index pays.</p>

   <h3>Building an index on a live table</h3>
   <p>Building an index on a large table takes time, and engines differ on whether writes can continue meanwhile. PostgreSQL’s plain <code>CREATE INDEX</code> blocks writes until it finishes. Its <code>CONCURRENTLY</code> option keeps them flowing at the price of a longer build, which <a href="/learn/databases/relational/postgresql/indexes/#concurrently">Indexes in PostgreSQL</a> explains. InnoDB builds a secondary index in place and keeps the table writable by default, and the <code>ALGORITHM</code> and <code>LOCK</code> clauses of <code>CREATE INDEX</code> let you require that. SQLite allows one writer at a time, so a build holds up every other writer until it finishes.</p>

   <h3>Finding unused indexes</h3>
   <p>An index no query reads still costs every write. PostgreSQL’s <code>pg_stat_user_indexes</code> view counts each index’s scans, and MySQL’s sys schema has a <code>schema_unused_indexes</code> view listing indexes with no recorded use. SQLite keeps no usage statistics, so there you check the plans of your queries. In PostgreSQL:</p>
   <Sql code={usage} label="Count how often each index on a table has been scanned" />
   <p>An index whose <code>idx_scan</code> stays at zero over a representative period is a candidate for removal. Each server keeps its own counts, so check every replica too, and make sure the counters haven’t been reset recently. An index that enforces a primary key or unique constraint stays, whatever its count. MySQL’s view carries the same warning about uptime: it means little until the server has run a representative workload.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="check">
   <h2>Checking an index with EXPLAIN</h2>
   <p>Databases can show the plan they chose for a query: <code>EXPLAIN</code> in PostgreSQL and MySQL, <code>EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN</code> in SQLite. The details below are PostgreSQL’s, and a table after them gives the equivalent terms in MySQL and SQLite. <code>EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)</code> in PostgreSQL also runs the query and reports what each step did and how many pages it touched. Here <code>ANALYZE</code> means run and measure; the separate <code>ANALYZE</code> command collects the planner’s statistics. Since PostgreSQL 18, <code>ANALYZE</code> reports pages without <code>BUFFERS</code>, but older versions need it.</p>
   <Sql code={explain(history)} label="Run the history query and report its plan" />
   <p>With the covering index in place and the table vacuumed, the important part of the plan looks like this, simplified: costs, timings, a type cast and one detail line are left out, and long lines are wrapped.</p>
   <pre class="plan"><code>{plan}</code></pre>
   <p>Read it from the most indented line outwards. The index-only scan produces rows in order, and <code>Limit</code> stops asking for more after ten. Four parts of a plan tell you what an index did:</p>
   <ul>
    <li><strong>The node type.</strong> <code>Seq Scan</code>, <code>Index Scan</code>, <code>Index Only Scan</code> or <code>Bitmap Heap Scan</code> names the route.</li>
    <li><strong><code>Index Cond</code> against <code>Filter</code>.</strong> Conditions under <code>Index Cond</code> were used in the index search. Conditions under <code>Filter</code> were checked on rows after fetching them, and <code>Rows Removed by Filter</code> counts the wasted fetches.</li>
    <li><strong><code>Heap Fetches</code>.</strong> In an index-only scan, the rows that still needed a table visit.</li>
    <li><strong><code>Buffers</code>.</strong> The <code>shared hit</code> and <code>read</code> counts are pages found in memory and pages read from storage. A temporary table, like this one, reports them under <code>local</code> instead.</li>
   </ul>
   <p>A paid-only version of the history query produces a <code>Filter</code>:</p>
   <Sql code={residual} label="Ask for customer 42’s ten newest paid orders" />
   <p>The index has no <code>status</code>, so PostgreSQL fetched 12 rows and discarded 2 pending ones to return 10. For a customer whose recent orders were mostly pending, the scan would read much further. An index on <code>(customer_id, status, placed_at DESC)</code> would move the condition into <code>Index Cond</code>, at the cost of splitting each customer’s history by status.</p>
   <p>An index is doing its job when the plan shows your condition under <code>Index Cond</code>, not <code>Filter</code>, and <code>Heap Fetches</code> near zero.</p>
   <p>MySQL and SQLite report the same things in different words:</p>
   <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-labelledby="explain-caption"><table><caption id="explain-caption">Where PostgreSQL, MySQL 8.4 and SQLite report the route a query took</caption><thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>PostgreSQL</th><th>MySQL 8.4 <code>EXPLAIN</code></th><th>SQLite <code>EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN</code></th></tr></thead><tbody>
    <tr><td>Was the whole table read?</td><td><code>Seq Scan</code></td><td><code>type</code> is <code>ALL</code> (<code>index</code> reads the whole index instead)</td><td><code>SCAN t</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Was an index searched?</td><td><code>Index Scan</code>, <code>Bitmap Heap Scan</code></td><td><code>type</code> is <code>ref</code> or <code>range</code>, with the index in <code>key</code> (<code>const</code> or <code>eq_ref</code> for a primary or unique key)</td><td><code>SEARCH t USING INDEX i (a=?)</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Which conditions did the index use?</td><td><code>Index Cond</code>; the rest under <code>Filter</code></td><td><code>Using index condition</code> tests conditions on index entries before reading rows; <code>Using where</code> tests them afterwards</td><td>The terms in parentheses, such as <code>(a=?)</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Did the index cover the query?</td><td><code>Index Only Scan</code></td><td><code>Extra</code> says <code>Using index</code></td><td><code>USING COVERING INDEX</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>How many pages were read?</td><td><code>Buffers</code>, with <code>BUFFERS</code></td><td>Not reported; <code>EXPLAIN ANALYZE</code> gives rows, loops and time</td><td>Not reported</td></tr>
   </tbody></table></div>
   <p>Without page counts, MySQL and SQLite let you check the route but not the arithmetic of this article, so on those engines the measurements here show what to expect, not what you will see. SQL Server’s <code>SET STATISTICS IO</code> does report pages, as logical reads from cache and physical reads from disk. The rule holds in every engine: an index search on the condition you care about, and a covering index where you expect one.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="run">
   <h2>Where the numbers come from</h2>
   <p>Every measurement here was taken in PGlite 0.5.8, a WebAssembly build of PostgreSQL 18.3, the same engine the site’s SQL sandbox runs in your browser. <a href="/learn/databases/relational/sandbox/?tutorial=indexes">Count the pages an index saves</a>, a guided sandbox lesson, builds the 60,000-order table and repeats each measurement in article order, with the number to expect at every step.</p>
   <p>To run them on your own PostgreSQL server, <a href={experimentUrl} download="index-experiment.sql">download the experiment script</a> and run it with <code>psql -f index-experiment.sql</code>. Page counts may differ slightly, and none of the numbers are timings. None of them come from InnoDB or SQLite, whose behaviour is described from their documentation.</p>
  </section>

